#{[17592186129603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129603 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129603 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcfe0e97-f702-5d83-9f81-8d21ad944be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129603 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7(\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1061 20099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129963 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1061 20100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129964 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1061 20101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129965 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129966 #edb/ref 1061 20102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129966 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129966 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1061 27265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1065 3566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130347 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1064 "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1065 4432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130403 #edb/ref 1067 "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Mean of a Continuous Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192675 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d3da4448-71cc-5671-8f32-a9f293ad5054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Continuous Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192676 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8322a1ba-7608-53de-88d9-e360ca8025cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Mean of a Continuous Random Variable With a Piecewise PDF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192677 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6b84b8fb-5ad7-5c4f-af44-b6d1dc9310c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1045 "Expected Values of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192901 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "555c23df-016c-5847-bcc4-15704704dfb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193493 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193493 #edb/ref 1061 29818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193493 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49df91fa-7474-5cd7-bce7-3052e981b2e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193493 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193494 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193494 #edb/ref 1061 29819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193494 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5f5c529-09c9-5bd8-8f07-58cf47f6162e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193494 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193495 #edb/ref 1061 29820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193495 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f67c50e7-8c03-5eaf-85c4-84bd537eb4d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193495 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193496 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193496 #edb/ref 1061 29821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193496 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ce5d9b1-f0eb-5bc1-9dae-be9875f2c61f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193496 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193814 #edb/ref 1064 "The **expected value** (also known as the **expectation** or **mean**) of a continuous random variable is the average value we'd expect to get if we observed the random variable many times.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193814 #edb/ref 1065 4804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193814 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "727440ce-9bc9-544c-889f-00c2107d4977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193814 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263025 #edb/ref 1080 "4{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263025 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263025 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02c7eef7-3d78-5989-86e0-94ecf6198c5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263096 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17